---
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import ToolPage from "@bt/ui/ToolPage.astro";
import AdSlot from "@bt/ui/AdSlot.astro";
import AguinaldoCalc from "../../components/AguinaldoCalc.tsx";
import { aguinaldo, MX_2026 } from "@bt/tools/mx";

const mxn = new Intl.NumberFormat("es-MX", { style: "currency", currency: "MXN" });
const exempt = Math.round(MX_2026.uma.daily * 30 * 100) / 100;
const minDaily = 315.04;
const bySalary = [minDaily * 30, 10000, 12000, 15000, 20000, 30000].map((m) => {
  const a = aguinaldo({ dailySalary: m / 30, daysWorked: 365 }).fullYearAmount;
  return { m, d: m / 30, a, ex: Math.min(a, exempt), tax: Math.max(0, Math.round((a - exempt) * 100) / 100) };
});
const dayMs = 86_400_000;
const byStart = [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11].map((mo) => {
  const days = Math.round((Date.UTC(2026, 11, 31) - Date.UTC(2026, mo, 1)) / dayMs) + 1;
  return { mo, days, a: aguinaldo({ dailySalary: 400, daysWorked: days }).proportionalAmount };
});
---
<Layout
  bleed
  lang="en"
  title="Mexico aguinaldo calculator 2026 (Christmas bonus) | Baitaan"
  description="Work out your 2026 aguinaldo from your salary and the days you worked. Legal minimum of 15 days, art. 87 of Mexico's Federal Labor Law. Free, no sign-up."
>
  <ToolPage
    heading="Aguinaldo calculator 2026 (Mexico)"
    lead="In Mexico the minimum aguinaldo, the year-end bonus, is 15 days' pay for a full year worked, paid in proportion if you worked less."
    updated="Updated: November 2026"
    sourcesHeading="Sources"
    sources={[{ href: "https://www.diputados.gob.mx/LeyesBiblio/pdf/LFT.pdf", label: "Federal Labor Law, article 87" }]}
  >
    <AguinaldoCalc client:load lang="en" />

    <Fragment slot="guide">
      <h2>How the aguinaldo is calculated</h2>
      <p>Multiply your daily salary by the aguinaldo days (at least 15). If you did not work the whole year, divide by 365 and multiply by the days you did work.</p>
      <p><strong>Formula:</strong> daily salary × aguinaldo days × days worked ÷ 365.</p>

      <h2>Example</h2>
      <p>With a monthly salary of $12,000, the daily salary is $400. For 182 days worked: 400 × 15 × 182 ÷ 365 = <strong>$2,991.78</strong>.</p>

      <h2>When is the 2026 aguinaldo paid?</h2>
      <p>The law says <strong>before 20 December</strong> (LFT article 87). In 2026 the 20th is a Sunday, so the real deadline is <strong>Saturday 19 December</strong>; many employers pay on Friday the 18th or with the first December payday. It can be paid earlier, in one or more parts, as long as the full amount is paid before the 20th.</p>

      <h2>2026 aguinaldo by salary</h2>
      <p>A full year at the legal minimum of 15 days. The aguinaldo is free of ISR up to 30 UMA ({mxn.format(exempt)}); anything above that is added to your income for the month to work out ISR, so what reaches your account can be a little lower.</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="text-muted">
              <th class="py-2 text-start font-medium">Monthly salary</th>
              <th class="py-2 text-end font-medium">Daily salary</th>
              <th class="py-2 text-end font-medium">Aguinaldo</th>
              <th class="py-2 text-end font-medium">Tax-free</th>
              <th class="py-2 text-end font-medium">Taxable</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {bySalary.map((r, i) => (
              <tr class="border-t border-hair">
                <td class="py-2 tabular-nums">{mxn.format(r.m)}{i === 0 && <span class="text-muted"> (minimum wage)</span>}</td>
                <td class="py-2 text-end tabular-nums">{mxn.format(r.d)}</td>
                <td class="py-2 text-end font-semibold tabular-nums">{mxn.format(r.a)}</td>
                <td class="py-2 text-end tabular-nums">{mxn.format(r.ex)}</td>
                <td class="py-2 text-end tabular-nums">{mxn.format(r.tax)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted">Rule of thumb: with 15 days, a full year's aguinaldo is half your monthly salary.</p>

      <h2>If you started work in 2026</h2>
      <p>You get the proportional part. On a salary of $12,000 a month ($400 a day):</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="text-muted">
              <th class="py-2 text-start font-medium">Start date</th>
              <th class="py-2 text-end font-medium">Days worked in the year</th>
              <th class="py-2 text-end font-medium">Aguinaldo</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {byStart.map((r) => (
              <tr class="border-t border-hair">
                <td class="py-2">1 {["January","February","March","April","May","June","July","August","September","October","November","December"][r.mo]}</td>
                <td class="py-2 text-end tabular-nums">{r.days}</td>
                <td class="py-2 text-end font-semibold tabular-nums">{mxn.format(r.a)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Common cases</h2>
      <ul class="ms-5 list-disc space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Resigning or dismissed:</strong> the proportional part is paid in your <a class="text-link underline" href="/en/calculadora-finiquito/">finiquito</a>, whatever the reason you left.</li>
        <li><strong>Part-time:</strong> it is worked out on your actual daily salary, like anyone else's.</li>
        <li><strong>Overtime, bonuses and commissions:</strong> the legal minimum uses your ordinary daily salary, without overtime. If your contract or employer gives more days or includes other pay, that applies.</li>
        <li><strong>Domestic workers:</strong> they are entitled too, with the same 15-day minimum.</li>
        <li><strong>Federal government workers:</strong> a different law applies and they get 40 days: half before 15 December and half by 15 January.</li>
        <li><strong>Freelance (honorarios):</strong> there is no aguinaldo. But if you are an employee in practice (set hours, a boss, a fixed fortnightly payment), you can claim it.</li>
      </ul>

      <AdSlot unit="0000000000" label="Advertisement" />

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>Is the aguinaldo taxed?</summary><p>It is tax-free up to 30 UMA ($3,519.30 in 2026); ISR applies to the rest.</p></details>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>Do I get an aguinaldo if I resigned?</summary><p>Yes, the proportional part is included in your <a class="text-link underline" href="/en/calculadora-finiquito/">finiquito</a>.</p></details>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>Do I get an aguinaldo if I didn't work the full year?</summary><p>Yes. You are owed the part that matches the days you worked in the year, even if you no longer work there on the payment date (art. 87 of the LFT).</p></details>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>How much is the aguinaldo on the 2026 minimum wage?</summary><p>On the general minimum wage of $315.04 a day, a full year's aguinaldo is <strong>{mxn.format(minDaily * 15)}</strong> (15 days). If you worked less, use the calculator with your days.</p></details>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>Can it be paid in vouchers or in kind?</summary><p>No. The aguinaldo is part of your salary and must be paid in money.</p></details>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>Do sick leave and holidays count?</summary><p>Holidays count as days worked. Sick leave is treated differently depending on its type; if your employer deducted days, check your case with PROFEDET.</p></details>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>What if my employer doesn't pay it?</summary><p>Ask the company in writing and, if it still doesn't pay, go to PROFEDET, which helps workers free of charge. You have one year to claim it (art. 516 of the LFT).</p></details>
    </Fragment>
  </ToolPage>
</Layout>
